# Read-only frequency discovery.  Generic CPU topology remains on AOSP's
# sysfs_devices_system_cpu so apps retain the standard read-only contract.
#
# All eight CPUs carry a cpufreq directory. An earlier revision of this file
# labelled only cpu0 and cpu1, on the strength of an `ls` taken while MTK's
# hotplug governor had six cores parked -- a cpufreq directory disappears with
# its CPU. With every core online:
#     cpu0: affected_cpus = 0 1 2 3      (cluster 0, LITTLE, max 1001000)
#     cpu4: affected_cpus = 4 5 6 7      (cluster 1, big,    max 1508000)
# and cpu1..cpu3, cpu5..cpu7 each expose the same policy through their own
# directory. The old labelling therefore covered one CPU of cluster 0 twice
# and left the whole big cluster on the generic type, so libpowerhal's
# /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu%d/... reads and writes fell outside the policy.
# Label all eight -- but only the WRITABLE ones.
#
# scaling_available_frequencies is deliberately NOT relabelled. It is read-only,
# and system/sepolicy/public/domain.te:210 already gives every domain
#   r_dir_file(domain, sysfs_devices_system_cpu)
# so hal_power_default can read it as sysfs_devices_system_cpu without any rule
# here. Moving it to a device type removed that platform-wide grant from
# system_server, dumpstate, incidentd, perfprofd, hal_thermal_default and every
# app, and put nothing back.

